| 0 | 0 | [0] | [1, 2] | 2 | false |
| 1 | 1 | [1, 2] | [3] | 1 | false |
| 2 | 1 | [2, 3] | [4] | 1 | false |
| 3 | 2 | [3, 4] | [5] | 1 | false |
| 4 | 2 | [4, 5] | [] | 1 | false |
| 5 | 3 | [5] | [] | 0 | true |

Predecessors: [6, 0, 0, 1, 2, 3] (sentinel 6). Layer counts: [1, 2, 2, 1]. Maximum queue length: 2.
\image(figures/bfs_queue_path.svg)
// result_end
\caption(Marking on enqueue prevents parallel routes from creating repeated queue entries. Edge 4–5 appears earlier in the input list than 3–5, but vertex three is processed first.)
\alttext(A directed graph splits into routes zero-one-three-five and zero-two-four-five. Both have three edges. The first route is highlighted because vertex three discovers the goal before vertex four is removed.)

> Why first discovery gives a shortest-hop distance

The start has distance zero. When a vertex at distance k is removed, each
new neighbour is assigned distance k+1 and appended to the queue's tail.
All earlier entries remain ahead of those discoveries. Inductively, dequeue
distances never decrease, and a vertex cannot first receive distance k+1 if
a shorter path exists: its predecessor on that shorter path would already
have been removed and would have discovered it earlier.

Store the first-discovery predecessor once and follow those links backwards
from the goal to reconstruct a minimum-edge path. This solves a unit-cost
problem. It does not minimise arbitrary edge weights. Different input orders
can select different equally short paths while preserving the hop distance.
Loops and parallel edges are allowed; they are inspected but cannot re-enqueue
vertices whose distances have already been assigned.

> Original example and stopping-time controls

The original chain has three vertices and edges 0→1→2. Its returned path has
three vertices and two edges. Input is directed by default; setting directed
false inserts both orientations. An unreachable goal returns an empty path,
while start=goal returns the single starting vertex after its dequeue.

The final control shows why the trace's distance sentinel means undiscovered
at stopping time. For edges 0→1, 0→2, 1→3 and 2→4, seeking goal two removes
zero, one and two. Vertex three has already been discovered at depth two and
remains queued. Vertex four is still undiscovered because the goal's outgoing
edge is not expanded, even though four is reachable in the full graph.

```{cpp, results="markdown", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  const auto original=dm::algorithms::breadth_first_search(3,{{0,1},{1,2}},0,2);
  std::print("Original path: {}; vertex count {}; edge count {}.\n\n",
      original.path,original.path.size(),original.path.size()-1);
  struct Example {std::string name;std::size_t n;std::vector<std::pair<std::size_t,std::size_t>> edges;
    std::size_t start,goal;bool directed;};
  const std::vector<Example> examples{
    {"original directed chain",3,{{0,1},{1,2}},0,2,true},
    {"reverse directed chain",3,{{0,1},{1,2}},2,0,true},
    {"reverse undirected chain",3,{{0,1},{1,2}},2,0,false},
    {"start equals goal",3,{{0,1},{1,2}},1,1,true},
    {"loops, duplicates, unreachable goal",4,{{0,0},{0,1},{0,1},{1,1},{1,2},{2,0}},0,3,false},
    {"goal pop leaves a deeper discovery",5,{{0,1},{0,2},{1,3},{2,4}},0,2,true}};
  std::print("| case | found | path | dequeue order | discovered distances (n = sentinel) | edge inspections |\n"
             "| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | ---: |\n");
  for (const auto& example:examples) {
    const auto trace=dm::algorithms::breadth_first_search_trace(example.n,example.edges,example.start,example.goal,example.directed);
    std::print("| {} | {} | {} | {} | {} | {} |\n",example.name,trace.result.found,trace.result.path,
        trace.result.order,trace.distance,trace.edges_examined);
  }
}
```
