| case | found | path | dequeue order | discovered distances (n = sentinel) | edge inspections |
| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | ---: |
| original directed chain | true | [0, 1, 2] | [0, 1, 2] | [0, 1, 2] | 2 |
| reverse directed chain | false | [] | [2] | [3, 3, 0] | 0 |
| reverse undirected chain | true | [2, 1, 0] | [2, 1, 0] | [2, 1, 0] | 3 |
| start equals goal | true | [1] | [1] | [3, 0, 3] | 0 |
| loops, duplicates, unreachable goal | false | [] | [0, 1, 2] | [0, 1, 1, 4] | 12 |
| goal pop leaves a deeper discovery | true | [0, 2] | [0, 1, 2] | [0, 1, 1, 2, 5] | 3 |
// result_end
\caption(Stopping on removal preserves the historical traversal order. The goal can be discovered before it is removed; other queued vertices may be processed in between.)

Predecessor sentinel n is used for the start and for undiscovered vertices.
Distance sentinel n is not a valid finite hop distance: any shortest simple
path has at most n-1 edges. If the goal is unreachable, queue exhaustion does
explore every vertex reachable from the start. Every discovered
vertex then has its full-graph minimum distance, and all other vertices are
unreachable. Both terminals and every edge endpoint must lie in [0,n);
an empty graph therefore has no valid call. Validation still checks every
edge when start=goal.

> Grid layers and complete adjacency work

The existing native grid generator labels cell (r,c) as r*cols+c and connects
horizontal/vertical neighbours. From the upper-left corner, the minimum-hop
distance is r+c: each step changes at most one coordinate by one, and a path
with r downward and c rightward moves attains that lower bound.

Use an 8 by 10 grid and request the native full traversal from its upper-left
corner. This entry point exhausts all reachable vertices without a target.
The histogram counts actual discoveries at each distance. Full queue snapshots
default off for this linear-cost traversal; queue lengths and discovery events
are still recorded.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/bfs_grid_layers.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  const std::size_t rows=8,cols=10,vertices=rows*cols;
  const auto edges=dm::algorithms::grid_graph_edges(rows,cols);
  const auto trace=dm::algorithms::breadth_first_traversal(vertices,edges,0,false);
  for (std::size_t r=0;r<rows;++r) for (std::size_t c=0;c<cols;++c)
    if (trace.distance[r*cols+c]!=r+c) return 1;
  if (trace.goal_requested || trace.vertices_enqueued!=vertices || trace.edges_examined!=2*edges.size()) return 1;
  std::vector<double> depth,count;
  std::print("| depth | discovered grid vertices |\n| ---: | ---: |\n");
  for (std::size_t d=0;d<trace.layer_counts.size();++d) {
    depth.push_back(d);count.push_back(trace.layer_counts[d]);std::print("| {} | {} |\n",d,trace.layer_counts[d]);
  }
  std::print("\nGrid vertices: {}; input edges: {}; adjacency entries inspected: {}; maximum queue: {}.\n",
      vertices,edges.size(),trace.edges_examined,trace.max_queue_size);
  dm::graphics::RPlot::create().bars(depth,count,"discovered vertices",dm::RGB{37,99,235},.8)
      .title("An 8 by 10 grid: actual shortest-hop layer counts")
      .x_label("minimum edge distance from the corner").y_label("discovered grid vertices")
      .y_limits(0,12).size(900,480).save_svg("build/bfs_grid_layers.svg");
  std::print("\n| grid side | reachable vertices | input edges | actual adjacency inspections | 2E reference |\n"
             "| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n");
  for (std::size_t side:{4,8,16,32}) {
    const auto n=side*side;const auto grid=dm::algorithms::grid_graph_edges(side,side);
    const auto work=dm::algorithms::breadth_first_traversal(n,grid,0,false);
    if (work.edges_examined!=2*grid.size() || work.result.order.size()!=n) return 1;
    std::print("| {} | {} | {} | {} | {} |\n",side,work.vertices_enqueued,grid.size(),work.edges_examined,2*grid.size());
  }
}
```
