\caption(The undirected rectangular grid has rows*(cols-1)+cols*(rows-1) input edges and twice that many stored adjacency entries. Full traversal inspects each entry once. Counts describe logical work, not elapsed time.)
\alttext(Layer sizes rise from one to eight, remain eight for the middle layers of the rectangle, then fall to one at the opposite corner.)

> Time, memory and the cost of a trace

Each vertex is enqueued at most once and removed at most once. Scanning a
removed vertex examines its adjacency entries, including duplicates and loops;
the goal's entries are excluded because removal stops the search. Validation
and graph construction still inspect the complete input edge list. Thus total
work is O(n+E), with E directed entries or 2E entries for undirected input.

Queue, distance, predecessor and result arrays use O(n) storage. Because this
API accepts an edge list and builds its own adjacency lists, total owned
working storage is O(n+E), rather than only the search arrays' O(n). The
ordinary function allocates no trace events. A trace without queue snapshots
adds only linear event storage: total discoveries are at most n-1.

By default, a trace also copies the active queue before every removal. If Q
is the sum of those queue lengths, this adds O(Q) time and storage; a wide
star can make Q quadratic. Passing capture_queue=false preserves the linear
bound while retaining actual lengths, discoveries, distances and counters.
All returned data are owned, inputs are unchanged, and independent calls share
no mutable state. Generated graphs provide the hand-checkable structural
examples here; no observational dataset is required.

> References

- E. F. Moore (1959), The shortest path through a maze, Proceedings of the
  International Symposium on the Theory of Switching, Part II, 285–292,
  Harvard University Press.
- C. Y. Lee (1961), An Algorithm for Path Connections and Its Applications,
  IRE Transactions on Electronic Computers EC-10(3), 346–365.
  [Original paper](https://doi.org/10.1109/TEC.1961.5219222),
  [primary-paper copy](https://janders.eecg.utoronto.ca/1387/readings/lee.pdf).

This implementation uses explicit unweighted adjacency lists and FIFO discovery;
it does not implement every routing objective discussed in Lee's paper.
